---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import SignatureGenerator from '@/components/tools/generators/SignatureGenerator.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="signature-generator">
  <SignatureGenerator client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to create your signature</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Sign in the box above. A finger on a phone or tablet gives the most natural result, a stylus is better, and a mouse takes a few attempts.</li>
      <li>Undo removes the last stroke, for example when the tail of a letter goes wrong. Ctrl+Z does the same from the keyboard.</li>
      <li>Set the pen width and ink colour. Dark grey looks more like real ink on a scanned page than pure black.</li>
      <li>Download the transparent PNG for documents and PDF forms, or the SVG for a web page or email footer.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Your strokes exist as coordinates in this browser tab, and the signature generator renders them into
      the file you save. When you close the tab they are gone, and no account holds a copy of your signature.
    </p>

    <h2>Why the line varies in thickness</h2>
    <p>
      A signature with a constant line width looks like a wire diagram. Real ink thins as the pen speeds up
      and pools where it slows or changes direction. The canvas models this in two ways: when the device
      reports real stylus pressure, it uses that; if not, it calculates the width from the speed between
      points and smooths it so the line does not shake.
    </p>
    <p>
      The path is a series of quadratic Bézier curves through the midpoints between sampled points, with each
      raw point as the control point. This standard technique for freehand input removes the angular look of
      straight segments and keeps the line where you drew it. To get a uniform stroke, turn off the variation
      in the pen panel.
    </p>

    <h2>PNG or SVG?</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th></th>
            <th>Transparent PNG</th>
            <th>SVG</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Scales without blurring</td><td>No: fixed resolution</td><td>Yes, to any size</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical size</td><td>20–80 KB</td><td>3–15 KB</td></tr>
          <tr><td>PDF form fields</td><td>Yes</td><td>Rarely accepted</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Word and Google Docs</td><td>Yes</td><td>Partially</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Email signature</td><td>Yes</td><td>Blocked by many clients</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Web page</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes, and sharper</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Recolour after export</td><td>No</td><td>Yes, edit the stroke attribute</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The PNG is rendered at three times the on-screen size, so it stays sharp at normal scale in a document.
      Trimming is on by default: it crops the empty space, so the image fits onto a signature line without a
      wide transparent margin.
    </p>

    <h2>Adding a signature to a document</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>PDF: open the file in a PDF editor, choose the image or stamp tool, place the PNG over the signature line, then flatten the document before you send it.</li>
      <li>Word: Insert → Picture, then set Wrap Text to “In Front of Text” so you can move it freely over the line.</li>
      <li>Google Docs: Insert → Image → Upload from computer, then choose “Behind text” wrapping.</li>
      <li>Email footer: use the PNG at about 200 px wide and always set explicit width and height attributes, because some clients ignore CSS sizing.</li>
      <li>Web page: use the SVG inline. It is a few paths, it inherits <code>currentColor</code> if you remove the stroke attribute, and it stays sharp on any display.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Is an image of your signature legally binding?</h2>
    <p>
      Not by itself. The US ESIGN Act and the EU eIDAS regulation both recognise electronic signatures, but
      what makes one valid is the evidence around it: proof that the signer intended to sign, proof that they
      agreed to sign electronically, an audit trail that links the signature to that person and that
      document, and a copy kept by both parties. A PNG has none of that.
    </p>
    <p>
      A drawn signature is widely accepted for internal approvals, delivery notes, timesheets, school forms
      and most everyday paperwork. For important contracts, such as employment, property or anything you may
      need to enforce, use a service that records the audit trail, or sign on paper. eIDAS also defines
      “advanced” and “qualified” electronic signatures. They need certificate-based identity verification,
      and only they have the same legal standing as a handwritten signature across the EU.
    </p>

    <h2>Keeping your signature safe</h2>
    <p>
      After export, anyone can copy a signature image. Protect it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Do not email the image file on its own. Send the signed, flattened document.</li>
      <li>Flatten PDFs before you send them, so the image cannot be extracted as a separate layer.</li>
      <li>Do not post a signed document publicly. Scanned contracts on company websites are a common source of forged signatures.</li>
      <li>Keep the master file somewhere private, and draw a new one from time to time.</li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
